COOK={
'32-01':('I bid all of you farewell.','**Grēte** uses the farewell sense of grētan with accusative **ēow ealle**. Ealle agrees with the persons addressed; it is not an adverb meaning entirely. This direct farewell meets the named lexical target without adding a separate bidding construction.'),
'32-02':('When they had rowed about twenty or thirty furlongs, they saw Jesus walking on the sea.','**Rēowon** is a simple preterite with anterior meaning supplied by the sequence; English had does not require an Old English perfect here. **Hǣlend** is the accusative actor of **gān**, and the infinitive stays last as directed. The supplied approximation and quantity phrase retains both twenty and thirty; **uppan ðǣm sǣ** uses dative agreement with the selected masculine sea noun. “Walking” in English does not make gān a participle.'),
'32-03':('He arose, commanded the wind, and said to the sea, “Cease.”','All three actions remain: **ārās**, **bebēad**, **cwæð**. The wind is the dative recipient **ðǣm winde**, while the sea follows **tō**. **Geswīc** is the singular absolute command to cease; it needs no genitive complement here. Substituting an accusative wind or treating the command as a past statement changes the structure.'),
'32-04':('The wind ceased, and a great calm arose.','**Geswāc** is absolute cessation. **Wearð** means came about, with nominative **miclu smyltnes**; there is no participle and therefore no passive auxiliary construction. **Miclu** is the selected feminine nominative strong form. The English indefinite article is omitted as instructed.'),
'32-05':('You hear his voice and do not know where he comes from or where he goes.','**Wāst** is the selected second-singular present of witan; separate **ne** negates it. The two dependent questions retain **hwanon … cymð** and **hwider … gǣð**. Use the uncontracted form supplied by the taught system; an invented negative contraction does not improve compliance. The voice **stefne** is accusative object of hear.'),
'32-06':('He saw two brothers casting their net into the sea; they were fishermen.','**Twēgen brōðor** is the accusative actor phrase of perception **sęndan**. Their **nętt** is the singular object sent; **on ðone sǣ** gives the goal. The finite **wǣron** belongs to the second clause. A finite sending clause could express related content, but it would replace the selected perception-infinitive construction.'),
'32-07':('He told them, “Come after me, and I will make the two of you fishers of men”; they immediately left their nets.','**Him** is the dative recipient of the saying. The result is a finite **ðæt** clause: nominative dual **git** takes plural **bēoð**, and **fisceras** is its nominative predicate. Do not replace git with an accusative object of dōn; this is the taught make-that construction. The model omits a will word as required, retains genitive **manna**, and uses plural **nętt** without an invented -u ending. The following-person phrase and immediately remain explicit.'),
'32-08':('If your hand or foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and cast it away; it is better for you to enter life lame than to have two hands and two feet and be cast into eternal fire.','Singular **ǣswicað** agrees with the alternatives hand or foot; **ðē** is the offended person, accusative. **Hit** resumes the alternative body part, and **of** is the off particle. The second command omits it as directed. In the supplied better-clause, **ðē** is dative; keep **ðæt** before the first complement, omit it after **ðonne**, and retain **gā**, **hæbbe**, **sīe worpen**. The final words are a passive; worpen is the selected prior participle.'),
'32-09':('If a man has a hundred sheep and loses one, does he not leave the ninety-nine in the mountains and go to seek the one that was perishing?','Keep the five present actions distinct: **hæfð**, **forlīest**, **forlǣtt**, **gǣð**, **sēcð**. The final **forwearð** is a preterite in the identifying relative; the English was perishing does not require a progressive auxiliary. **Hund scēapa** has the supplied quantity construction, and **ān of ðǣm** selects one from the set. The question keeps **ne** but has no equivalent of auxiliary does.'),
'32-10':('If he happens to find it, truly I tell you that he rejoices more over that sheep than over the ninety-nine.','The first **hit** in the supplied **gif hit gelimpð** is impersonal; the second is the sheep. **Finde** is present subjunctive, not indicative findað. **Swīðor** compares the degree of rejoicing; both objects retain **ofer** with accusative. Replacing the comparison with a statement of preference would lose this use of more.'),
'32-11':('When he comes home, he calls his friends and neighbours together and says, “Rejoice with me, because I found my lost sheep.”','**Hām** expresses homeward motion without an added preposition. Singular **cleopað** summons the friends; plural **Blissiað** addresses them. The quoted first-singular preterite is the course-selected **fand**, omitting have as directed. **Forloren** is strong accusative neuter singular with **mīn … scēap**; the participial base does not license an arbitrary -e ending. The documented first-singular funde is a linguistic variant, but differs from the selected cell.'),
'33-01':('Going into the house, they found the child with Mary, his mother.','The whole **intō ðǣm hūse gangende** phrase is supplied; gangende accompanies the plural actors, while **fundon** is finite. **Bearn** is the accusative child. **Mid Marian his mēder** retains the supplied name form and the dative relation, with his mother identifying Mary. Do not turn the going into an unrelated extra finite subject.'),
'33-02':('Arise, take the child and his mother by night, flee to the land of the Egyptians, and remain there until I tell you.','Preserve all the commands and both people taken. **Ārīs**, **nim**, **flēoh**, **bēo** address one person. The supplied **on niht** gives the time, while **tō Egypta lande** gives the destination. **Sęcge** is the finite subjunctive in the until clause and must stand last there; it is not another command to the hearer.'),
'33-03':('He arose, took the child and his mother by night, and journeyed into the land of the Egyptians, so that what the Lord spoke through the prophet might be fulfilled: “From the Egyptians I called my son.”','The selected narrative forms are **ārās**, **nōm**, **fōr**. **On Egypta land** is a goal, unlike the time expression on niht. The purpose has **gefylled wǣre**, with no separate might word; the thing fulfilled is the spoken utterance. **Fram dryhtne** and **ðurh ðone wītgan** retain their distinct cases. The quoted **cleopode** is first singular without have. A separately evidenced travel verb could express journeyed, but no new fēran system is required by this unqualified action.'),
'33-04':('What can they ask for?','**Hwæs** is genitive: the thing requested is governed by **biddan**. **Magon** is plural and governs the plain infinitive. Do not substitute accusative **hwæt** for the genitive thing requested merely because English what has no visible case ending. The same request frame can also take an accusative person, as in36.8.'),
'33-05':('The sun rules over the day.','**Gīemð** is singular, and its rule/govern sense takes genitive **ðæs dæges**. This is lexical government, not a general rule that everything following an English over must be genitive. The already taught gīeman frame meets Cook’s §156f target.'),
'33-06':('They expected nothing except death itself.','**Wēndon** means expected, with a genitive thing expected: **nāhtes**. The supplied exceptive phrase keeps **dēaðes selfes** in the corresponding genitive relation. **Ne** participates in the negative statement. A finite that-clause is another construction of wēnan, but does not explain the genitives required here.'),
'33-07':('You will cease from your work.','**Geswīcst** is present second singular with the future meaning supplied by context. **Ðīnes weorces** is the genitive complement of cease. A willan paraphrase may express futurity elsewhere, but the selected simple present already fulfils the task; adding an English-shaped from phrase would miss the requested genitive government.'),
'33-08':('Perhaps he will find a friend.','The taught **Wēn is ðæt** pattern expresses expectation or possibility, followed by present subjunctive **finde**. **Frēond** is the accusative object found. The English will is not translated automatically by willan, which would introduce an intention not required by this model.'),
'33-09':('He said to them, “I will go fishing.” They replied, “And we will go with you.”','The quotation changes from **ic wille** to **wē willað**, retaining each speaker’s own viewpoint. The supplied activity phrase is **on fiscnoð**; the companions follow **mid** in **mid ðē**. Do not leave the second willan singular merely because the first quotation was singular.'),
'33-10':('They went out, went into a ship, and caught nothing that night.','Both movements and the failed catch remain finite past events. **On scip** is the goal, whereas **on ðǣre niht** is the supplied time expression. **Ne … nāwiht** gives one negative meaning, not two cancelling negatives. The uninflected-looking **niht** is the selected dative here; do not invent nihte to make its case more visible.'),
'34-01':('Whoever you are, do not forsake me.','**Swā hwā swā** introduces free choice with subjunctive **sīe**. **Ne forlǣt** is a singular negative command and takes accusative **mē**. The -e of sīe does not make it an infinitive, and the negative command needs no auxiliary equivalent to English do.'),
'34-02':('If you find nobody who will feed you, return here.','**Nānne** is the possible person found, with negative **ne**; hypothetical **finde** is present subjunctive. The relative **ðe ðē fēde** describes a possible provider, so fēde is also subjunctive, with the fed person accusative. Singular **ęfthweorf** is the command to return. The two subjunctives express this context; they are not a universal translation of English will.'),
'34-03':('Let my books suffice for both of us.','The complete **Genyhtsumien … unc bǣm** construction is supplied. **Mīna bēc** is nominative plural, so the optative/subjunctive is plural **genyhtsumien**. **Unc** is dual dative; **bǣm** is dative plural because both has no separate dual table. Cook’s verified noun is books; the cited reading has possessions and provides the construction, not a replacement for the exercise noun.'),
'34-04':('Both boys are well educated.','**Bēgen ðā cnapan** and predicate **gelǣrede** agree in the masculine plural. Here gelǣred is the taught lexical adjective educated or trained. Do not infer a separately assigned finite gelǣran conjugation from its participial origin. Well trained is an appropriate contextual rendering of the same adjective.'),
'34-05':('They were both righteous before God.','**Bēgen** and **rihtwīse** are plural predicates referring to hīe; **wǣron** supplies the past tense. **Beforan Gode** has a dative complement. Translating both by a dual personal pronoun would change they into we two or you two rather than supplying the required agreeing word.'),
'34-06':('I solemnly charge you by the living God to tell us whether you are Christ, the Son of God.','**Hālsie** takes accusative **ðē** and the oath phrase **ðurh ðone libbendan God**. That participial adjective is supplied as a weak accusative masculine form. **Ūs** is the dative recipient of telling; **sęcge** belongs to the request clause. **Gif … sīe** gives the whether-content. Do not confuse the charged person’s accusative with the speech recipient’s dative.'),
'34-07':('I tell you: after these things you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of God’s majesty and coming in the clouds of heaven.','One accusative actor, **Mannes Sunu**, controls both perception infinitives **sittan** and **cuman**. English sitting/coming may therefore be rendered without participles. The supplied right-hand phrase retains Cook’s expressly required accusative **on ðā swīðran hand**; do not change it to a motion meaning just to explain the case. **On heofones wolcnum** is a separate locative phrase. The model omits the specified things and articles.'),
'34-08':('May it be for you two according to your faith.','**Sīe** expresses the wish without inserting the omitted it. **Inc** is dative dual, while **bǣm** agrees in its available dative plural form. **Incrum** preserves the dual possessive reference. The supplied **æfter … gelēafan** means according to, rather than following someone in space.'),
'34-09':('Truly the Son of Man is to come in his Father’s glory and will then reward everyone according to his own work.','Both **is tō cumanne** and the complete reward clause are visibly supplied. The former is this bounded be-to construction, not merely the previously taught purpose after a coming verb. **Cumanne** is the selected form; manuscript evidence also has cumenne. **C06:** Cook’s printed hint **āgyltan,113** is corrected to **āgyldan**, strongIII, for reward/repay. Its contracted third singular **āgylt** is supported, and **ǣlcum** is the dative recipient. Keep the original hint identifiable, but do not manufacture a weak reward form from it. No shall word is added.'),
'34-10':('When Christ comes, no one then knows where he is from.','The temporal pair is **ðonne … ðonne**. **Ne wāt nān mann** is a single negative statement; wāt is the selected third-singular present, with a dependent whence-question. A future interpretation of comes does not require a separate future auxiliary or an unassigned contracted negative-witan form.'),
'34-11':('Jesus cried out and taught in the temple: “You know me and where I am from. I did not come from myself; the one who sent me is true, and you do not know him.”','The model retains crying, teaching and saying. **Cunnan** has an accusative person; **witan** has the whence-content. In **sē … ðe mē sęnde**, the sender is nominative subject; in **ðone gē ne cunnon**, the same person is accusative object. **Of mē selfum** keeps the dative self-phrase after of. The supplied predicate **sōð** describes the sender as true; it is not an adverb modifying sent.'),
'34-12':('You have not known him; I know him. If I say that I do not know him, I will be like you; but I know him and keep his saying.','The first clause uses the requested preterite **cūðon** without have. Reported **cunne** is subjunctive and final in its clause; the surrounding **cann** forms are indicative. **Bēo** expresses the future without shall, and **ēow** is dative with like. **Healde his sprǣce** uses keep/observe with an accusative saying, rather than the earlier physical hold sense.'),
'34-13':('If I glorify myself, my glory is nothing; my Father is the one who glorifies me, and you say concerning him that he is your God.','**Mē selfne** is accusative reflexive. The following negative clause begins with **nis**, satisfying the verb-first control, and **nāwiht** is its supplied predicate. **Be ðǣm** means concerning whom. The final reported **sīe** is subjunctive. Cook allows a g-containing weak-II spelling series, but the selected first singular is **wuldrie**, not an unnoticed mixture of spellings.'),
'35-01':('Catch this ball.','**Fōh** is the singular command. **Ðisne ðoðor** is accusative masculine singular; the demonstrative agrees with the ball’s grammatical gender. The source’s rare ball word is a noun, not a finite verb inferred from its ending.'),
'35-02':('Who caught the black ball?','**Hwā** is the subject and **fēng** the third-singular preterite. The object **ðone blacan ðoðor** has weak accusative masculine adjective agreement after the demonstrative. Present catch and preterite caught require different fōn stems; the question does not change that distinction.'),
'35-03':('Go along the way by which you came.','The route is genitive **ðæs weges**, the pattern directly encountered in the reading. **Gā** is the singular command; **cōme** is preterite indicative second singular in the relative clause. Its final -e alone does not make it subjunctive. The selected genitive target should not be replaced by an unverified accusative route merely because English has no preposition.'),
'35-04':('I never played better than I did today.','**Plegode** expresses ordinary playing. **Dyde** substitutes for that same action and has first-singular agreement. **Bęt … ðonne** compares actions, with **tō-dæg** at the current speaker’s viewpoint. A repeated playing verb could form a valid comparison, but the selected model practises substitute dōn. **Ne … nǣfre** is one negative assertion.'),
'35-05':('She never sang better than she did now.','**Dyde** repeats the singing action, this time in the third singular. Keep **nū**, the now of the narrated viewpoint, rather than mechanically changing it to then. The comparative is adverbial **bęt**. Repeating sang could convey the comparison, but would not display the selected substitute-action pattern.'),
'35-06':('Three times I suffered shipwreck; I was in this sea for a night and a day.','**Ðrīwa** counts the sufferings; **forlidennesse** is what was suffered. The time extents **āne niht** and **ānne dæg** are accusative. **On ðissum sǣ** is locative dative with the selected masculine noun. Do not make a night and a day two new subjects of wæs.'),
'35-07':('I knew a certain man who was carried to the third heaven and heard there words that no earthly man may speak.','**Wiste** takes the accusative person **sumne mann**; knowing people is not restricted to cunnan. **Wæs … lǣded** is a passive carrying event, with the supplied **ōð** endpoint. The relative hearer is the same man. In the final clause **nān … ne mōt** expresses prohibition, and **sprecan** is its plain infinitive. Translating it as a positive permission would reverse the meaning.'),
'35-08':('How narrow is the gate and the way that leads to life, and very few are those who find the way.','The complete Old English **hū nearu is ðæt geat and se weg** is supplied with its source-shaped singular agreement. It treats the figurative route as a unit and is not a rule for every coordinated subject. **Lǣtt** stays final in its relative clause. Supplied **fēawe** is plural and takes **sindon**; **swīðe** preserves very, and the requested there is omitted.'),
'35-09':('They come to you in sheep’s garments, but inwardly they are wolves.','**Cumað** is a present plural statement, not a command. **On scēapa gegierelum** is the supplied clothing expression: scēapa is genitive plural and gegierelum dative plural. **Innan** supplies the inward contrast. Treating on as a destination here would change clothes into a place they enter.'),
'35-10':('When he approached the city gate, a dead man was being carried there.','The first **ðā** means when, the second then, and **ðǣr** means there. **Nēalǣcte** governs dative **geate**; **ðǣre byrig** is its genitive city dependent. **Wæs … boren** is passive **beran**, as requested. English being carried is a natural rendering of that event; it does not require an additional Old English progressive verb.'),
'35-11':('I see a girl running along the street.','Accusative **mǣden** is the actor of perception infinitive **iernan**. **Geond ðā strǣte** takes accusative and identifies the space traversed. The model uses the selected Cook headword mǣden, without an added g, and the accusative strǣte rather than the nominative strǣt.'),
'35-12':('Anoint me with oil.','**Smiere** is the selected singular command, with accusative **mē** and material **mid ęle**. The spelling **smire** is independently attested and can be valid Old English; the selected table keeps ie. Do not omit the singular -e merely because other commands such as dēm lack it.'),
'35-13':('Give us some of your oil.','The selected model addresses several people: **Sęllað** and **ēowrum** agree in that reference. **Ūs** is the dative recipient, and supplied **of ēowrum ęle** is partitive, not departure from a place. For one addressee, the already taught singular command and possessive give **Sęle ūs of ðīnum ęle**, using the same partitive help. Keep command and possessive reference consistent. This is an alternative to the selected plural model; the English itself does not spell out singular versus plural you.'),
'35-14':('If the Son frees you, you will indeed be free.','**Ālīest** is third singular; **ēow** is the plural person freed, and **gē bēoð** preserves that plural reference without shall. The predicate plural **frige** is supplied and directly supported. The dictionary headword frēo is not the selected plural answer.'),
'36-01':('Do not judge according to appearance; judge rightly.','Before the following subject **gē**, the selected plural command is shortened **dēme**. In the second clause, without a following subject pronoun, the ordinary plural is **dēmað**. Both remain commands to several people. **Be ansīene** is the supplied according-to expression; **rihtne dōm** is the accusative cognate object, “right judgment.” Cook’s positional target is specific; do not claim that a different historical variant could never occur.'),
'36-02':('He turned to the woman and said, “Do you see this woman? I entered your house; you gave me no water for my feet, but she washed my feet with her tears.”','**Bewęnde hē him** has the specifically taught dative reflexive, and **tō ðǣm wīfe** is the goal. **Gesiehst** is the selected second-singular form. The supplied feminine **ðēos** refers to the woman as a person despite the grammatical neuter wīf. **Ðwōh** is third-singular past; **mīne fēt** is its accusative object and **mid hiere tēarum** the washing liquid. Keep the separate supplied intended-use phrase **wæter tō mīnum fōtum**; it is not another movement destination.'),
'36-03':('You gave me no kiss; since I entered, this woman has not stopped kissing my feet.','The first clause ends in **sealdest**, as directed, and contains no indefinite article. **Inēode** is the selected first-singular past of ingān. The entire **ne geswāc ðæt hēo mīne fēt ne cyste** is supplied from the attested negative cessation construction. Both negatives belong to it: the inner ne does not mean she refused to kiss. Do not add a have auxiliary or invent a general rule for every complement of cease.'),
'36-04':('You did not anoint my head with oils; this woman anointed my feet with ointment.','Keep the instructed plural **ęlum** and singular **sealfe**. Second-singular **smieredest** has -st; third-singular **smierede** does not. The objects hēafod and fēt are accusative, and both materials follow mid in the dative. Attested i/y spellings can be linguistic alternatives, but cannot remove the person distinction.'),
'36-05':('Therefore I tell you that many sins are forgiven her, because she loved me greatly.','**Synna** is the plural passive subject, **hiere** the dative person forgiven, and **forgiefen** an uninflected predicative participle. It is not a finite plural verb. The opening supplied **For-ðon** means therefore; **for-ðon-ðe** introduces the reason. Keep **lufode** last in that clause and retain swīðe. Merely knowing forgiefan as give/grant would not establish this forgiveness sense.'),
'36-06':('The person to whom less is forgiven loves less.','The complete compressed relative sentence is supplied. The first **lǣs** is the prompt’s neuter accusative degree expression, “less” with loves. The second **lǣs** is nominative neuter singular: the smaller amount that is forgiven. **Ðǣm ðe** is dative within the forgiveness relation, with the matrix subject understood. This is not evidence that lufian normally takes a dative subject. An expanded relative could express the meaning, but would replace the supplied compact target.'),
'36-07':('You are not permitted to have her as your wife.','The whole permission frame is supplied: impersonal **hit ne ālīefð**, dative permitted person **ðē**, and **hīe** as accusative object of **habban**. **Tō wīfe** expresses her role as wife, not a destination. The English is is omitted because the finite permitting verb already supplies the predicate; do not add wesan.'),
'36-08':('He then promised with an oath to give her whatever she asked him for.','Use the supplied promise/free-choice bridge. **Hiere** is the dative giving recipient and resumptive **ðæt** the accusative thing given. Inside **swā hwæs swā**, biddan requires genitive **hwæs** for the thing requested and accusative **hine** for the person asked. **Bǣde** is preterite subjunctive; the omitted English should does not become another verb. One English whatever cannot force the same case into two different constructions.'),
'36-09':('Prompted by her mother, she said, “Give me John the Baptist’s head on a dish.”','Supplied **myngod** is an uninflected predicative participle; **fram hiere mēder** supplies its agent. Singular **Sęle** has dative **mē** and accusative **hēafod**. Supplied **Johannes** is genitive here, with matching **ðæs fuluhteres**. The primary name spelling has initial I; the declared J follows Cook’s headword. That spelling choice is separate from case.'),
'36-10':('He then sent and beheaded John in the prison.','The source leaves the sending patient unexpressed; the model preserves that bounded absolute **āsęnde**. **Behēafdode** is third-singular past and takes supplied accusative **Johannem**. The prison is locative **on ðǣm cwearterne**. Do not keep the name in its dictionary nominative merely because English John shows no case ending, or silently replace the active beheaded with a different command construction.'),
'36-11':('Someone brought his head on a dish.','Indefinite **man** supplies the unnamed human agent, and **brōhte** is third-singular past. **His hēafod** is accusative object; **on disce** identifies the dish carrying it. English one means someone here, not a numeral modifying the head. This is a separate final narrated event and must not be omitted.')}
